  "evidence": "- TEXT_C (Labour): Unshakeable NATO commitment and UK nuclear deterrent; Strategic Defence Review and path to 2.5% of GDP on defence; tougher counter-espionage and counter-extremism; Martyn’s Law. Secure borders via a new Border Security Command, smashing smuggler gangs, fast-tracking returns, clearing the asylum backlog, and a UK–EU security pact. “Take back our streets” with a Neighbourhood Policing Guarantee, new offences (assaults on shopworkers), knife-crime bans and mandatory interventions, specialist RASSO units, fast-track rape courts, and prison/reoffending reforms. Strict fiscal rules with OBR oversight.\n- TEXT_E (Liberal Democrats): Reverse Army cuts and move towards 2.5% GDP on defence; maintain NATO and the nuclear deterrent. Restore community policing and guarantee burglary attendance; tackle court backlogs. But also scrap anti-protest laws, end disproportionate Stop and Search, end the Hostile Environment, scrap the Illegal Migration Act and Rwanda scheme, create more safe/legal routes, allow asylum seekers to work after three months, and pursue extensive devolution and electoral reform (PR, votes at 16).",
  "reasoning": "While both texts endorse NATO and higher defence spending, Labour centers authority-affirming measures—stronger borders, a new Border Security Command, tougher counter-espionage/extremism, expansive policing powers, new offences, and stricter institutional rules. The Liberal Democrat text places greater emphasis on civil liberties, rolling back police and protest powers, loosening the government’s coercive stance on immigration, and decentralising authority, which aligns less with a strong Authority/Subversion priority.",
